  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the **exact** value of $c$ that satisfy the mean value theorem for  $f(x)={x}^{2}-4x+2$ on the interval $[0,6].$\n\n$c=$ \n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,6]$ and differentiable on $(0,6),$  hence we can apply the mean value theorem.  \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,6)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=6$ into the above gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(c) & =\\frac{f(6)-f(0)}{6-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{({6}^{2}-4\\cdot 6+2)-({0}^{2}-4\\cdot 0+2)}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{14-2}{6} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}-4x+2)=2x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(c)=2c-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2c-4 & =2 \\\\ c & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
